source":"gtwo_kml_202611211743","idp_filedate":1795283003000,"nhcTitle":"Offshore of the Southeast U.S. Coast","discussion":"A non-tropical gale-force low pressure area centered several  hundred miles offshore of northeastern Florida is producing  disorganized showers and thunderstorms primarily along a stationary  front. This low is forecast to move northward into an environment  that will probably not be conducive for development of significant  tropical or subtropical characteristics.  1. Although the chances of tropical development remain low, this large  weather system is still expected to produce gusty winds, dangerous  surf, rip currents, and coastal flooding along portions of the  southeast and mid-Atlantic U.S. coasts into early next week. In  addition, heavy rainfall and the threat of flash flooding will move  northward over coastal portions of the Carolinas and the  mid-Atlantic through the weekend. Interests along the U.S. East  Coast should continue to monitor the progress of this system.  Additional information on this system, including Gale Warnings, can  be found in High Seas Forecasts issued by the National Weather  Service, as well as products issued by your local National Weather  Service forecast office. \n Formation chance through 48 hours...low...10 percent. \n Formation chance through 7 days...low...10 percent.","disturbance":1}}]}